# Project-level git ignore
.termlink-task
build/
tools/__pycache__/
tests/__pycache__/

# T-410: Watchtower's session signing key. It was tracked from 2b9c8ffa (2026-06-04)
# until T-410 — 2 months of commits, pushed to origin and mirrored to GitHub (D-701),
# carrying the LIVE key that signs fw_session_<port> and the CSRF token inside it.
#
# No leading slash, so this matches at ANY depth — deliberately. The key exists once
# per PROJECT_ROOT, and this tree holds two of them: ours at .context/working/ and the
# vendored framework's at .agentic-framework/.context/working/ (that second one was
# already sitting untracked-but-committable when this was found). A path-anchored rule
# would have covered one and left the other.
#
# `.context/working/.gitignore` also lists it. That file is the natural home, but it
# only governs its own directory — it cannot reach the vendored copy. Both, on purpose.
.fw-secret-key

# T-938: Watchtower's API-key store. SECOND INSTANCE OF THE T-410 CLASS ABOVE, same
# component, same project, three months later — which is why this block is worded as a
# pattern rather than a path.
#
# web/secrets_store.py (T-378) writes Fernet-encrypted API keys to
# .context/secrets/api-keys.enc INSIDE the consuming project's repo, and the framework
# never ignored that path. In this tree the file was TRACKED, committed in 973813ea
# (2026-09-27) and pushed to origin/bleeding-edge before anyone looked.
#
# ENCRYPTED IS NOT SAFE HERE, and this is the part that made it survive. The Fernet key
# is derived from /etc/machine-id, which is world-readable and routinely logged or
# copied, so the encryption protects the file only while it stays on this machine. In a
# pushed repo it is a usable key to anyone holding both. 055-agentic-fleet-cockpit put it
# best when they found the same gap (framework:pickup @229): "the encryption makes the
# file look safe to commit, which makes that mistake more likely, not less."
#
# No leading slash, same reasoning as .fw-secret-key: the store exists once per
# PROJECT_ROOT and this tree holds two roots, ours and the vendored framework's.
#
# T-410 fixed one key path and not the mechanism that writes secrets into a repo nobody
# ignored. This entry is the same local fix; the mechanism fix is AEF's and is filed with
# them, because a .gitignore line here leaves every other consumer exposed (PL-214).
.context/secrets/
*.enc

# runme.sh writes a timestamped log per run plus a LATEST copy, both at the project
# root (the standing operator instruction requires the log to open on the first line,
# before argument validation, so the refusal path leaves a trace). They are run
# artifacts, not source. Ignored so handing the operator a logging script does not
# also hand them a dirty working tree — which is how a real change gets lost in noise.
runme-*.log

# T-970: the Evergreen corpus intake. Their maps describe a real company's internal
# processes; the owner approved SENDING them to us, which is not the same as archiving
# them in our git history forever. Analysed in place, never committed.
build/evergreen-intake/
docs/learning-ledger.yaml.lock
